PROJECT: Refactor my QA dashboard's Release Health module from request-time Jira/qTest fetching to a production-grade background-sync architecture.

CURRENT STACK: FastAPI backend, [Postgres/MySQL — FILL IN], Jira REST API, qTest API, [frontend framework — FILL IN].

CURRENT PROBLEMS:
- Dashboard fetches Jira/qTest live per request → slow first loads, 429 rate-limit errors, API breaking under query volume.
- Backend throws "connection pool is full" errors under load.
- Must serve 200+ concurrent users (directors/CEOs) with zero visible slowness or errors.

====================================================
NON-NEGOTIABLE ARCHITECTURE PRINCIPLES
====================================================
1. ZERO Jira/qTest API calls in any dashboard request path. Dashboard endpoints read ONLY from local DB (or Redis cache).
2. A single background sync worker owns ALL outbound Jira/qTest traffic.
3. All heavy metrics (release readiness score, burndown, health) are PRECOMPUTED during sync and stored — never computed per request.
4. No request may ever hold a DB connection while waiting on any external I/O.
5. Failure of any external dependency (Jira, qTest, Redis) must degrade gracefully — never a broken page, never a 500 to the user.

====================================================
A) SYNC WORKER (freshness layer)
====================================================
- APScheduler-based sync job. Scheduler must run in EXACTLY ONE process — use a Redis leader lock so multiple gunicorn workers don't double-sync.
- Per active project: incremental sync every 3 min (1-2 min for projects with an active release). Jira JQL: project = X AND updated >= "-10m" (overlap window). qTest: last-modified filters. Upsert by Jira key / qTest ID.
- JIRA WEBHOOKS for near-real-time freshness: POST /webhooks/jira receives issue created/updated/deleted events, worker applies them to DB within seconds. Polling remains as fallback for missed webhooks. Validate webhook payloads; process async (background task), respond 200 immediately.
- Initial seed: one-time full paginated fetch per project, resumable via stored cursor/checkpoint, exponential backoff, honor Retry-After on 429.
- Global token-bucket rate limiter on ALL outbound Jira/qTest calls; timeouts on every call; circuit breaker so a Jira/qTest outage never hangs the worker or app.
- NIGHTLY FULL RECONCILIATION per project: compare local keys vs Jira/qTest key lists, remove/flag records deleted at source, log any drift found. Guarantees no permanent mismatch.
- sync_state table: per-project cursor, last_synced_at, status, error message.
- Structured logging per sync run: duration, items synced, API calls made, 429 count.

====================================================
B) SCHEMA
====================================================
- Normalized tables: jira_issues, jira_versions (releases), qtest_runs, qtest_cycles — indexed on project_id, fix_version, status, updated_at.
- release_metrics: one row per (project, release, sync_timestamp) with readiness_score, burndown datapoint, pass/fail counts, open defect counts. Appending per sync gives burndown history for free.
- Alembic migrations for all schema changes.

====================================================
C) REDIS (read-cache + coordination layer — NOT a freshness mechanism)
====================================================
- Cache-aside on dashboard read endpoints: key = endpoint+project+release+params, TTL 60s.
- INVALIDATION: on every successful sync/webhook-apply for a project, delete all Redis keys for that project (prefix pattern). Refresh-button syncs also invalidate. Redis must never be staler than DB.
- Redis failures non-fatal: if Redis is down, fall through to DB silently.
- Redis also used for: scheduler leader lock, refresh-endpoint debounce lock.
- NO Celery, no message queues — APScheduler + Redis is sufficient at this scale.

====================================================
D) API LAYER
====================================================
- All release-health endpoints: single indexed SELECTs against local tables / release_metrics. Target <100ms uncached, <20ms cached. No N+1 queries — verify with query logging.
- Server-side pagination on all large lists (execution history, issue lists).
- GET /sync/status/{project} → last_synced_at, sync state.
- POST /sync/refresh/{project} → immediate incremental sync; debounced via Redis lock (if sync <60s ago or in progress, return in-progress status).
- Per-user rate limiting (slowapi) so one misbehaving client can't starve others.
- Response compression (gzip/brotli).
- /health and /health/deep endpoints.

====================================================
E) CONNECTION POOL — ELIMINATE "pool is full" PERMANENTLY
====================================================
- Async DB driver only (asyncpg / SQLAlchemy 2.0 async). No sync DB calls anywhere in the event loop.
- ONE engine/pool per worker process created at app startup (lifespan), never per-request engine creation.
- Sessions acquired via FastAPI dependency with context manager — guaranteed release on every path including exceptions. Audit ALL existing code for connection leaks (sessions opened without close, engines created inside functions).
- Connections held only for the duration of the query — never across external I/O, sleeps, or long computation. (Architecture already guarantees no Jira calls in request path; enforce the pattern everywhere else too.)
- Pool sizing: per-worker pool_size ~10, max_overflow ~10, pool_timeout 30, pool_pre_ping=True, pool_recycle=1800. With 4-8 workers, ensure total (workers × (pool_size+overflow)) stays under the DB's max_connections — check and state the math explicitly.
- The listener/execution-ingest path (Robot Framework listener writes) must use the same pooled engine, not ad-hoc connections.
- Add pool metrics logging (checked-out connections, overflow, wait time) so pool pressure is visible before it errors.

====================================================
F) DEPLOYMENT & RUNTIME
====================================================
- gunicorn with 4-8 uvicorn workers.
- Graceful shutdown: scheduler stops cleanly, in-flight syncs checkpoint, pools disposed.
- Feature flag to keep old live-fetch path during rollout; remove after validation.
- All secrets/config via environment variables.

====================================================
G) FRONTEND HARDENING
====================================================
- "Last synced: X min ago" badge + Refresh button (disabled while sync in progress, polls /sync/status).
- Widgets load in parallel; skeleton loaders; per-widget error state with retry — one failed widget never blanks the page.
- Debounce project/release selector; cancel in-flight requests on change (AbortController).
- Retry with exponential backoff on failed calls.

====================================================
H) ACCEPTANCE GATES — NOT DONE UNTIL ALL PASS
====================================================
1. Load test (k6 or locust): 250 concurrent users, mixed endpoints, 10 min sustained → p95 < 300ms, error rate < 0.1%, ZERO pool-exhaustion errors in logs.
2. Kill Redis mid-load-test → dashboard keeps serving from DB, no user-facing errors.
3. Simulate Jira 429s/outage mid-sync → dashboard serves last-known data with stale indicator, worker recovers automatically.
4. Reconciliation test: delete an issue in Jira (or mock it) → nightly job removes it locally.
5. Grep/audit confirms zero Jira/qTest client calls in any request handler.

